world leaders at the g7 summit could be  ready to declare the raging wildfires  burning in the Amazon rainforest an  international crisis after dragging his  feet for a few days Brazil's president  appears to be responding to the  worldwide uproar by adding more boots to  the firefight Brazil contains about 60%  of the Amazon rainforest Kenneth Craig  is tracking the latest developments  Kenneth good morning well good morning  to you Dana the Amazon rainforest  produces a significant part of the  planet's oxygen and is a critical part  of containing climate change an  international outcry for a plan of  action has drawn brazil's president into  the spotlight and last night he  announced his plan in a televised speech  Brazilian president Jair bolson Oro says  he will be taking a tough approach to  battling the devastating fires burning  through parts of the Amazon bolson ro  who has reduced funding for  environmental agencies and relaxed  restrictions on logging promised to send  armed forces to the region to help douse  the flames wildfires are common in  Brazil during the country's dry season  which could last until November but  others are deliberately set by farmers  and loggers clearing the land but bolson  ro has suggested environmental groups  may be responsible for setting the fires  in retaliation for reduced funding from  his administration hundreds of  protesters marched the streets of Rio de  Janeiro to denounce the Brazilian  President as bolson ro announced the  military would join the fight against  the fires images of the fires have  spread around the globe sparking  protests outside Brazilian embassies in  South America and the United Kingdom and  international pressure is building on  Brazil from world leaders French  President Emmanuel macron plans to bring  Brazil's environmental crisis to the  forefront at the g7 leaders summit in  France this weekend Britain's Prime  Minister Boris Johnson and Germany's  Chancellor Angela Merkel have called the